  "country": "MX",
  "notes": "Mexico holds about 1,000 regular species and 110 endemics, and nobody visits the whole country, so the country trip keeps only the birds a visitor meets in several parts of it, and six region packs hold the rest. Hand-picked (manual) from GBIF's copy of the eBird dataset (occurrence.csv, November 2026): a bird goes in the country trip when it has at least 6,000 records and no single region (the six packs, grouped by state) holds half of them. That gives 291 species: the widespread residents of gardens, towns, farmland, wetlands and coast, plus the winter visitors from North America (warblers, orioles, waders, ducks). Mexico's own birds are mostly regional, so the trip has only six endemics (Rufous-backed Robin, Mexican Duck, Blue Mockingbird, Red Warbler, Orange-breasted Bunting, White-striped Woodcreeper); the other endemics sit in the packs. Birds with fewer than 1,200 records (800 for endemics) are in no pack: they are rare or very local. Saltmarsh Willet was left out for lack of a usable photo.",
